WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.400
My name is Rutger Bregman.

00:00:01.480 --> 00:00:05.480
I'm a historian,
and in my humble opinion

00:00:05.560 --> 00:00:08.880
bin men and women
should be paid more than bankers.

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.760
So a good way
to illustrate this point

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:15.320
is to look at a couple of strikes
that happened throughout history.

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:19.360
So for example in New York, 1968,
there was a big strike of bin men.

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:22.760
They were just angry that their wages
were not going up anymore

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.800
so they said, 'You know what,
we're gonna go on strike

00:00:24.880 --> 00:00:26.880
and then you'll see just
how important we are.'

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:29.640
And indeed, six days later,

00:00:29.720 --> 00:00:32.040
the state of emergency
had to be declared.

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:33.520
New York really couldn't handle it.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.680
Turns out you can't
do without bin men.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:41.000
Two years later there was another
strike, in this case of bankers.

00:00:41.080 --> 00:00:43.440
It's the only strike that
I know of throughout history

00:00:43.520 --> 00:00:45.000
where the bankers
actually went on strike.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:47.720
And all the experts at that point,
all the economists,

00:00:47.800 --> 00:00:50.080
they all predicted disaster.

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.520
This was supposed to be like
a heart attack for the economy.

00:00:53.120 --> 00:00:54.720
But then the strike started.

00:00:55.240 --> 00:00:57.040
And nothing much happened really.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:01:00.200
It actually lasted
for six months in the end.

00:01:06.680 --> 00:01:09.720
the economy just kept growing,
businesses kept operating.

00:01:09.800 --> 00:01:12.000
There are nowadays
many people in professions

00:01:12.080 --> 00:01:14.280
in say Wall Street or Silicon Valley

00:01:14.640 --> 00:01:18.240
that are often described
as the visionaries and the whizkids

00:01:18.320 --> 00:01:20.040
who come up with
all these wonderful products.

00:01:20.120 --> 00:01:22.200
There was someone at Facebook
who worked there for years

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.360
and said, "the best minds
of my generation

00:01:24.440 --> 00:01:27.560
are thinking about how
to make people click ads."

00:01:27.960 --> 00:01:30.280
That's one of the tragedies
of our time

00:01:30.360 --> 00:01:33.400
is that we're wasting so much talent.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:35.720
There are so many
smart people right now

00:01:35.800 --> 00:01:37.720
in jobs that don't
contribute anything.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:40.120
And it's not necessary.
It's not inevitable.

00:01:40.200 --> 00:01:44.240
We need to rethink who the real
wealth creators actually are

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:45.280
in our society.

00:01:45.800 --> 00:01:48.200
So there's some
fascinating research actually,

00:01:48.280 --> 00:01:50.800
new research from
two Dutch economists.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:53.760
Max van Lent and Robert Dur who
looked at this whole phenomenon

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:55.000
of what they call...

00:01:57.400 --> 00:01:58.720
They asked people the question...

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:07.120
Turns out that in modern economies,
developed economies,

00:02:07.200 --> 00:02:09.480
25% of the workforce says...

00:02:15.480 --> 00:02:17.480
Now who are these people,
is an interesting question.

00:02:17.800 --> 00:02:20.840
Turns out that actually, they have
wonderful LinkedIn profiles.

00:02:20.920 --> 00:02:22.680
They have wonderful,
excellent salaries,

00:02:22.760 --> 00:02:25.120
they're often at the
top of the pyramid.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:28.720
They're bankers,
corporate lawyers, consultants.

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.520
Basically, you know, a lot of people
sitting in offices all day,

00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:34.400
sending emails
to people they don’t like,

00:02:34.480 --> 00:02:36.520
writing reports
no-one's ever going to read

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.440
but still making a lot of money.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:42.360
So that's quite fascinating right.
That the rest of the population

00:02:42.440 --> 00:02:45.520
is supporting this whole
class of people

00:02:45.600 --> 00:02:47.160
who don't really contribute anything.

00:02:47.360 --> 00:02:51.280
Right? What we're doing is
we're spending billions of pounds

00:02:51.360 --> 00:02:55.360
to educate our best and brightest
and they go to Oxford and Cambridge

00:02:55.440 --> 00:02:59.120
and then they go on and they do jobs
that they absolutely don't like.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:01.800
I mean, it's not me saying,
it's people themselves saying it,

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:03.600
where they don't
really contribute anything.

00:03:03.800 --> 00:03:06.400
What's going on here?
Who are the real wealth creators?

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:09.280
And the reality here is, I think,

00:03:09.360 --> 00:03:11.920
is that we're living in an
inverse welfare state.

00:03:12.240 --> 00:03:15.320
Where most wealth is
actually created at the bottom

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:16.960
by people who are doing
the real work.

00:03:17.040 --> 00:03:19.760
The teachers, the nurses,
the garbage collectors, you know.

00:03:20.000 --> 00:03:23.520
This state of affairs, it's not
inevitable, we can change it

00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:28.280
and I would like to live in a society
where we actually pay people

00:03:28.360 --> 00:03:30.600
according to their contribution.

00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:32.920
Where we have a real meritocracy.

00:03:33.280 --> 00:03:36.600
And in such a society
I believe in the long run

00:03:37.360 --> 00:03:40.000
bin men and women will be paid
more than bankers.

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:49.520
Thanks for watching. 
Don’t forget to subscribe! :)

